  "annotations": [
    {
      "id": "flow-note",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -240,
      "w": 940,
      "page": "page-1",
      "text": "Foundit MVP. Desktop web, every frame 1440×900, so pages scroll inside the frame like a browser tab.\nHomepage → Results (a chat you keep refining) → Tool detail. Nothing waits for approval: a tool is live the moment it is added.\nRow 2: the other states of Results. Row 3: Browse problems, Top tools, and the report flow, which emails the team rather than filling a queue."
    },
    {
      "id": "acct-note",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -200,
      "w": 940,
      "page": "page-2",
      "text": "Search and results never need an account. The gate appears only when someone saves, likes, reviews or adds a tool.\nSign in with Google, Apple, or a 6-digit code by email. The pending save completes straight after."
    },
    {
      "id": "maker-note",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -240,
      "w": 940,
      "page": "page-3",
      "text": "Two ways a tool gets here, and only two.\nA PERSON ADDS THEIR OWN. Step 13 is a single required tick — \"Yes, I made this tool\" — and it opens unticked, with Continue greyed out until it is on. It publishes at once and the listing stays theirs: they edit it, they get the dashboard, nobody can take it over. Recommending someone else’s tool is not in the MVP; that link goes to us.\nFOUNDIT SEEDED IT AT LAUNCH. These are the only claimable listings. Claiming is one click, no verification, no waiting — say it is yours and it is.\nNobody, maintainer included, can edit or delete a review."
    },
    {
      "id": "sys-note",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -160,
      "w": 520,
      "page": "page-4",
      "text": "Tokens and components for hand-off. The fit meter is the signature element; the logo is a pin-shaped bubble with a lens and a spark."
    },
    {
      "id": "later-note",
      "x": 0,
      "y": -240,
      "w": 940,
      "page": "page-5",
      "text": "Designed, deliberately out of the MVP. Build these when the catalogue is big enough to need policing.\nThe moderation queue, reviewing an edit from someone who does not own the listing, asking them for changes, merging two listings of the same tool, and a public change history.\nUntil then: owners edit their own listings directly, duplicates are caught the moment a tool is added, and reports go to the team by email."
    }
  ],
